\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat training split. Training uses 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42; ThreadGate additionally with seed 123); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (102\,GB). Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}; the controlled comparison design is described in \S\ref{sec:comparison-design}. We evaluate under \emph{IID} (same-distribution UltraChat text) and \emph{held-out} (50 non-overlapping conversations) teacher-forced protocols, reporting GAR-ours, GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}, and PPL.

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. Under TF evaluation, all four fine-tuned variants converge: GAR-ours spans 0.49--0.51 ($\leq$2\,pp), PPL is 2.78 for all. The Base model confirms LoRA fine-tuning as the operative variable (GAR-ours: 0.01$\to$${\sim}$0.50). Convergence near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 is expected given the shared GAR loss and does not demonstrate gating benefit. GAR-Dongre remains low (0.01--0.02); scheduled sampling breaks this pattern (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Under AR generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR-ours drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}a). ThreadGate significantly outperforms LoRA-only (Wilcoxon $p$\,=\,0.004, $r$\,=\,0.454; GAR-ours 0.064 vs 0.037; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-stats}), yet the residual gap remains large (7.8$\times$). A second seed (123) confirms TF stability ($0.504\pm0.001$). We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Five-way comparison on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. TF evaluation uses IID UltraChat and 50 held-out conversations; AR evaluation uses $N$\,=\,50 held-out conversations (mean 3.6 user turns). All fine-tuned variants converge under TF ($\leq$2\,pp spread); the 8--17$\times$ TF-AR gap persists across all configurations. $\bar{g}\!\pm\!\sigma_g$: learned gate mean and std across 28 layers (LoRA-only has no gates). GAR-O = GAR-ours; GAR-D = GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}.}
\label{tab:four-way}
\begin{tabular}{l ccc ccc cc c}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{IID (TF)}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Held-out (TF)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{AR ($N$\,=\,50)}} & \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7} \cmidrule(lr){8-9}
\textbf{Configuration} & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & 95\% CI & $\bar{g}\!\pm\!\sigma_g$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Baseline}} \\
Base              & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.23 & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.34 & 0.019 & {\scriptsize[.014,\,.024]} & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Gated variants}} \\
ThreadGate        & 0.50 & \textbf{0.02} & 2.78 & 0.50 & \textbf{0.02} & 2.78 & \textbf{0.064} & {\scriptsize[.041,\,.091]} & .93\,{\scriptsize$\pm$\,.05} \\
\;+\,PCGrad       & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.030 & {\scriptsize[.023,\,.038]} & --- \\
Uniform Gate      & 0.49 & 0.01 & 2.78 & \textbf{0.51} & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.033 & {\scriptsize[.025,\,.043]} & .96\,{\scriptsize$\pm$\,.05} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Ungated}} \\
LoRA-only         & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.037 & {\scriptsize[.027,\,.050]} & 1.00 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under TF evaluation ($N$\,=\,45, $\pm$1\,std). Fine-tuned variants maintain GAR near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 (7--8\% decay T1$\to$T4); Base decays 80\%. AR GAR collapses to 0.019--0.064 for all variants (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}).}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

All training-time interventions leave the exposure bias gap intact. We test inference-time recovery by applying additive bias $\beta$ to goal-token attention logits during generation (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}b). At $\beta$\,=\,3 ($N$\,=\,50), GAR recovers 8.7$\times$ (0.064$\to$0.556) with only 9\% PPL cost; at $\beta$\,=\,5, GAR reaches 0.727$\pm$0.061 (PPL 1.77). An initial sweep ($N$\,=\,5, 10-turn; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering}) reveals sharp nonlinearity at $\beta \in [2, 5]$ with ${\sim}$11$\times$ recovery.

This provides constructive evidence that attention misallocation---not representation degradation---is the proximal cause: manually correcting attention restores GAR, circumventing the exposure bias barrier. The experiment is diagnostic, requiring goal-token identification.

